Currently the OIDC-client implementation redirects all requests, if authentication is needed, to the configured OIDC service. This includes ajax-requests, which in my opinion can not be handled decently by the OIDC service.
I know that the problem with redirects being made from OIDC-client upon receiving an ajax request has already been discussed in issue WFLY-18889, but nevertheless i would like to propose a patch, which instead of redirecting the ajax request, simple reacts with a 403 forbidden error.
This way the application developer has the chance to intercept the ajax response on the client side and act accordingly (e.g. initiating a page reload).
The problem with the 302 redirect is, that the browser immediately takes care of the response and there is no chance that any client-side code for intercepting may be executed.
The tiny patch for the {color:#00627a}OidcRequestAuthenticator{color} would look like this:
{code:java}
diff --git a/http/oidc/src/main/java/org/wildfly/security/http/oidc/OidcRequestAuthenticator.java b/http/oidc/src/main/java/org/wildfly/security/http/oidc/OidcRequestAuthenticator.java
index 072fb5121..979d5700b 100644
--- a/http/oidc/src/main/java/org/wildfly/security/http/oidc/OidcRequestAuthenticator.java
+++ b/http/oidc/src/main/java/org/wildfly/security/http/oidc/OidcRequestAuthenticator.java
@@ -304,8 +304,12 @@ public class OidcRequestAuthenticator {
     protected AuthChallenge loginRedirect() {
         final String state = getStateCode();
         final String sessionRandomValue = generateSessionRandomValue();
+        // XMLHttpRequest should lead to an error immediately, since OIDC service cannot provide a decent response
+        // in this case
+        boolean xhr = facade.getRequest().getHeader(HttpConstants.X_REQUESTED_WITH) != null && facade.getRequest()
+                .getHeader(HttpConstants.X_REQUESTED_WITH).equalsIgnoreCase("XMLHttpRequest");
         final String redirect = getRedirectUri(state, Oidc.getCryptographicValue(sessionRandomValue));
-        if (redirect == null) {
+        if (redirect == null || xhr) {
             return challenge(HttpStatus.SC_FORBIDDEN, AuthenticationError.Reason.NO_REDIRECT_URI, null);
         }
         return new AuthChallenge() {
{code}
Maybe this change could be considered as a basis to overcome the problems with XMLHttpRequests and OIDC regarding the problematic redirects.

